[2025-09-23 20:29:17,371][Bodhi.src.Bodhi.parallel_kg_extractor][INFO] - Concise summary: 
This paper introduces ViCo, a method for learning word embeddings from visual co-occurrences extracted from large-scale, textually-annotated visual databases like VisualGenome and ImageNet. The core concept is to leverage multiple types of visual co-occurrences (e.g., object-hypernym, object-attribute) to create embeddings that capture visual concept similarities and differences better than text-only embeddings like GloVe.  ViCo is evaluated through unsupervised clustering, supervised partitioning, and zero-shot-like generalization, demonstrating improved performance when combined with GloVe. Key datasets include VisualGenome, ImageNet, and WordNet (used for synset annotations). The paper also explores the application of ViCo in vision-language tasks (e.g., caption-image retrieval, VQA) and compares it to existing methods like GloVe, vis-w2v, and random embeddings. The methodology involves log-bilinear models, co-occurrence matrices, and type-specific transformation functions. The paper also discusses the use of  Adagrad for optimization and analyzes the impact of WordNet and dimensionality on ViCo's performance.


